# Expert streaming's host-shared words (routed.seismic's streaming section):
# the one statement of every index and extent the host and the device agree
# on. routed.seismic names them, natives read them as `SEISMIC_CONST_<NAME>`,
# and the executor as `magnitude_kernels::constants::<NAME>`.

# Placement: the host is the one writer of every map entry, on every
# backend. Across a link it copies a record's bytes into the bank on the
# copy engine, off every step's path, then maps it (MAP_* below); a file
# miss lands into its tier (`tier[e]`). On unified memory it reads a file
# miss into a staging record, which the layer's landing launch converts
# into a bank record (the landing list, below), and maps it once the device
# is past that launch; there is no tier.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The streaming contract: the one statement of the
# words the device, the CPU pool and the host share for a streamed decode
# layer. Every word is an i32 word of a host-shared tensor (`Tensor::shared`).
#
# Steps. A step's sequence q is a u64. Every step the host and the device
# share across them (a map entry's MAP_FROM and MAP_UNTIL, an exchange
# slot's SLOT_REQUEST and SLOT_ANSWER) is one u64 at an even word (8-byte
# aligned, little-endian: the low word first), written as one 64-bit
# release store and read as one 64-bit acquire load (the device's
# `streaming_load_u64_acquire`, `streaming_store_u64_release`; the host's
# `Tensor::load_acquire_u64`, `store_release_u64`), never as two 32-bit
# accesses.
#
# The step word `step[STEP_WORDS]`: the step's sequence (`routed_step`, from
# the host's `sequence[STEP_WORDS]`, written before the step is submitted),
# its low word (STEP_LOW) then its high word (STEP_HIGH). The device writes
# and reads it in order within its queue; the words that tell steps apart
# only within a flight and that nothing waits on (routing records, hint
# rings, lists) carry its low word.
const STEP_LOW = 0
const STEP_HIGH = 1
const STEP_WORDS = 2

# The map. A layer's map is `map[E, MAP_WORDS]`, the layer's rows of its
# group's map `map[GM, MAP_WORDS]` from its home (`policy[POLICY_HOME]`):
# for each expert e, the first step it is valid for (MAP_FROM) and the
# first step it is invalid for (MAP_UNTIL, all ones while mapped), each one
# u64 (above; MAP_WORDS is even, so every row's steps are aligned), then the
# bank record holding it (MAP_RECORD, its own i32 word, -1 for none). The
# host is the only writer, and maps only records, so a mapped entry's record
# is a record; the device treats `record` as held for step q iff FROM <= q
# < UNTIL, and otherwise as not held.
# - Map: the host writes RECORD, then FROM (the first step not yet queued,
#   once the record's copy has completed), then UNTIL all ones, each with
#   release.
# - Unmap: the host writes UNTIL (the first step not yet queued) with
#   release; RECORD and FROM stay.
# - Reuse: the host writes a record's bytes, or maps it for another entry,
#   only once every step below the UNTIL of each entry naming it has ended,
#   by whichever exit, so the device runs none of them (the host's frontier,
#   `Steps::frontier`: the least step not yet ended).
# The device reads UNTIL with acquire, then FROM, then RECORD
# (`streaming_held`), so an UNTIL it reads new carries the FROM and RECORD
# before it.
const MAP_FROM = 0
const MAP_UNTIL = 2
const MAP_RECORD = 4
const MAP_WORDS = 6

# The tier map `tier[e]`: the host tier record holding expert e whole, or -1
# (host: set with release only while the record's bytes are whole, which the
# device reads with acquire; a record unmapped reused only once every step
# that could have resolved it has completed).

# A layer's policy `policy[POLICY_WORDS]` (host, between flights, the
# watcher's `publish_policy`): whether the layer's group is whole
# (POLICY_WHOLE, nonzero: every layer of it holds every expert at its home
# record, so nothing misses there and no route reads the map), the layer's home in its group's map (POLICY_HOME), then the
# lines the route's assignment reads, each in whole nanoseconds, the cost
# function's own (`assessment::cost`, `Policy::of`):
# - the CPU's, for a layer with a CPU site, from its site's lines: an
#   expert of r rows from RAM, POLICY_CPU_NS + (r − 1) POLICY_CPU_ROW_NS,
#   and the handshake a request adds (POLICY_HANDSHAKE_NS);
# - the GPU's: an expert of r rows read in place from the host tier,
#   max(POLICY_LINK_NS, r POLICY_LINK_ROW_NS), and from the bank,
#   max(POLICY_HIT_NS, r POLICY_HIT_ROW_NS).
# POLICY_PRICED is set once every line the assignment reads is measured;
# until then no line is written nor read, and a layer without a site never
# reads the CPU's. Last, whether no expert of the layer can be absent
# (POLICY_RESOLVED, nonzero: its group is whole, or its group's host tier
# keeps every expert), so no step's route can request the file and the
# landing launch (`routed_land`) is not encoded, nor its park where parks
# are submission boundaries.
#
# The assignment (`assign` in routed.seismic), per step on the device, is
# the cost function's (`ExpertLines::serve` at one step's choices, nothing
# copied, every record whole at the router), stated once here: each
# distinct expert's rows r are the choices naming it. The GPU's queue
# starts at its hits, the sum over the distinct hit experts of their
# resident lines; the CPU's at zero. The distinct missed experts (those the
# tier holds; across a link also those it lacks, which land in it first)
# are taken by rows, most first, ties in choice order; each goes to the CPU
# when it ends there no later than in place (its line, plus the handshake
# for the CPU's first), else to the GPU in place. Until POLICY_PRICED is set
# (every line it reads measured, a site's CPU lines among them) no miss is
# assigned: every one is the GPU's in place, the CPU site off. Every choice
# of an expert goes to its expert's side.
const POLICY_WHOLE = 0
const POLICY_HOME = 1
const POLICY_PRICED = 2
const POLICY_CPU_NS = 3
const POLICY_CPU_ROW_NS = 4
const POLICY_HANDSHAKE_NS = 5
const POLICY_LINK_NS = 6
const POLICY_LINK_ROW_NS = 7
const POLICY_HIT_NS = 8
const POLICY_HIT_ROW_NS = 9
const POLICY_RESOLVED = 10
const POLICY_WORDS = 11

# The exchange: the one protocol by which the device
# asks the host for anything and waits for its answer. Every request is a
# slot of a table of its kind; step q's slot of a table of S slots is slot
# q mod S. The host queues step q + S only once step q has ended, so a slot
# is the step's alone from its request until its answer is read, and
# answers never cross. A slot's words:
# - SLOT_REQUEST, a u64: the step the slot is requested for, stored by the
#   requester last, after the request's payload, with release;
# - SLOT_ANSWER, a u64: the step, or the step with SLOT_FAILED set, stored
#   by the answerer last, after the answer's payload, with release; it only
#   moves forward (an answer for a step no later than the one it holds
#   leaves it), so a late answer never overwrites a later step's;
# - SLOT_PULSE, an i32 the host's pulse advances every PULSE_MS while the
#   host lives (below), in every slot of every table;
# - SLOT_VIOLATED, an i32: the low word of the step in which a kernel found
#   a state the protocol never reaches, with VIOLATED_STAMP set (so a zeroed
#   word never matches a step), stored by that kernel alone, which the host
#   panics on;
# - SLOT_EXPIRED, a u64: the step whose wait on the slot ended on its still
#   pulse before its answer, stored by that waiting kernel alone (below);
# - from SLOT_PAYLOAD, the kind's payload (each kind below).
# The one wait rule, every wait's on every backend (`slot_answered` in
# routed.seismic, which the device's `streaming_await_slot` and the host's
# `exchange::ExchangeTable::answered` compute): step q's wait on its slot is over
# once the slot's request is not q (nothing was asked, so nothing is
# awaited), or its answer is q or q | SLOT_FAILED. A wait reads an answer's
# payload only after the answer, and only on the step's answer, never on
# its failure. Every request is answered, by its step or by its failure,
# while the host lives; a stuck host is the host watchdog's to abort.
#
# A host that is gone answers nothing, and a spinning wait is the one
# kernel with no end of its own. Metal does not end a dead process's
# running kernel: the process ends, but its GPU stays busy and its memory
# held until a reboot (a streamed step stopped in decode on an M4 Pro,
# 2026-11-02; NVIDIA's driver ends it within a second). So the host proves
# it lives: its load's pulse (`exchange::Pulse`), which every table joins
# as it is made, advances every slot's SLOT_PULSE every PULSE_MS, and a
# spinning wait ends, as on its step's failure, once that word has not
# moved for PULSE_SPINS of its spins, at the device's own cost of a spin:
# at least 100 ms at the fastest spin measured (70 ns, an idle M4 Pro),
# under a second on a Mac (535 ns, its slowest), and longer across a
# discrete device's bus, whose spins read host memory over it (a 3090's and
# a 4070's waits outlasted 1.2 s of still pulse). The patience is counted
# per single poller: those costs are of one reader of the host's words, so
# a launch of many threadgroups polls through one of them at a time
# (`streaming_land_poll`, streaming/land.hpp); many readers contending for
# a discrete device's bus would each spin many times slower, past any
# bound the pulse is stated in.
# A live host meets that end only if its pulse starves for that long (a
# loaded machine): the wait then stores its step as the slot's SLOT_EXPIRED,
# and the host interrupts that step, its outputs unused and its seeds never
# mapped, and re-runs it from its accepted state (the scheduler's round
# driver); a late beat no wait outlasted, or a shorter stall, touches
# nothing. How long a live host's answer may take is the
# watchdog's alone (its bound from the load's lines).
#
# How a backend waits is the backend's one fact (`SEISMIC_PARKS_IN_KERNELS`,
# from `BackendName::parks_in_kernels`): Metal and CUDA spin in the waiting
# kernel; Vulkan, whose running work sees no host write, waits at a park
# before the waiting call, a submission boundary the host opens once the
# rule holds (the executor's `watcher::parks`). A wait whose request is its
# own arrival (a scratch round's) stores its request as it begins: in its
# kernel, or on Vulkan by the host as the device reaches its park, where
# every write the device made before it is the host's to read.
const SLOT_REQUEST = 0
const SLOT_ANSWER = 2
const SLOT_PULSE = 4
const SLOT_VIOLATED = 5
const VIOLATED_STAMP = 2147483648
const SLOT_EXPIRED = 6
const SLOT_PAYLOAD = 8
# The failure bit of an answer, bit 63: bit 31 of its high word.
const SLOT_FAILED = 2147483648
# The host's pulse period, and the spins a wait sees its pulse still before
# it ends (above).
const PULSE_MS = 10
const PULSE_SPINS = 1500000

# The site kind: a sited layer's misses the route gave the CPU
# (`routed_site_request`, then the combine's wait), each a tensor of the
# layer:
# - `site[FLIGHT, R]` (R >= SITE_ENTRIES + SITE_ENTRY_WORDS M K, even), the
#   slots. The request's payload: the count of entries (SITE_COUNT), then
#   each entry from SITE_ENTRIES: the decode row it serves (SITE_ROW), the
#   host tier record the CPU computes it from (SITE_TIER_RECORD), the expert
#   it routed to, whose per-expert scales the CPU applies (SITE_EXPERT), and
#   its routing weight's f32 bits as the combine applies it
#   (SITE_WEIGHT_BITS). An expert two rows chose is two entries. The CPU
#   pool answers it.
# - `site_rows[FLIGHT, DECODE_ROWS, H]` f32, the request's payload too: the
#   layer's expert input rows, widened to f32 exactly.
# - `site_output[FLIGHT, DECODE_ROWS, H]` f32, the answer's payload: for
#   each row, the weighted sum over its entries of the CPU's expert outputs.
# A step with no CPU entry requests nothing and waits for nothing. The
# combine adds the output on the step's answer, and nothing on its failure
# (the host fails exactly the rows that named a failed expert).
const SITE_COUNT = 8
const SITE_ENTRIES = 9
const SITE_ROW = 0
const SITE_TIER_RECORD = 1
const SITE_EXPERT = 2
const SITE_WEIGHT_BITS = 3
const SITE_ENTRY_WORDS = 4

# A decode step's sources at a layer, the route's snapshot
# `sources[SOURCE_ROWS, M, K]` every later entry of the layer reads: each
# choice's record the GPU reads (SOURCE_RECORD: a bank record >= 0, a tier
# record t read in place as -2 - t, or -1: the GPU computes nothing for the
# choice), and the host tier record the CPU computes it from (SOURCE_SITE,
# or -1). A choice is the GPU's whole or the CPU's whole.
const SOURCE_RECORD = 0
const SOURCE_SITE = 1
const SOURCE_ROWS = 2

# The file kind: a decode layer's experts neither its bank nor its tier
# holds (file misses, which land first), its table `file[FLIGHT, FW]` (FW >=
# FILE_ENTRIES + FILE_ENTRY_WORDS P, even; P the decode form's widest
# choices), the slots. The payload: the count of choices (FILE_COUNT), then
# each choice's entry from FILE_ENTRIES, FILE_ENTRY_WORDS words: the
# request's word (FILE_CHOICE, `routed_route_streamed`'s), an absent
# expert as itself, any other as -1 - expert; and for an absent choice the
# answer's (FILE_RECORD, the host's, which `routed_land` reads), the record
# its expert lands in (a bank record r on unified memory, which the landing
# launch converts it into from staging; a tier record t as -2 - t across a
# link, which the host converted it into; -1 for a failed read). The host
# answers each expert's choices as its read lands, and the slot once every
# absent expert's are. A step with no absent choice requests nothing.
const FILE_COUNT = 8
const FILE_ENTRIES = 9
const FILE_CHOICE = 0
const FILE_RECORD = 1
const FILE_ENTRY_WORDS = 2

# A landing list `landing[LANDING_ENTRIES + LANDING_ENTRY_WORDS L]`
# (host-shared, the host's `landing_extent`): the records one landing
# launch converts as their reads land, a decode layer's (`routed_land`, its
# file misses) or a scratch round's (`routed_round`, its conveyed records),
# L its most entries.
# - LANDING_HEAD, one u64 at an even word: its low word the step's low word
#   (STEP_LOW), its high word the count of entries landed. The host stores
#   it with one 64-bit release store after each entry it appends, in
#   landing order (the order its reads land, not the record order); the
#   device reads it with one 64-bit acquire load. A head whose low word is
#   another step's counts none, so a step's first entry needs no head
#   before it, and within a step the count only grows.
# - From LANDING_ENTRIES, LANDING_ENTRY_WORDS words an entry: the record it
#   converts from (LANDING_FROM: a staging or conveyor record >= 0, or in a
#   round a negative from naming scratch slot -1 - from, which the host
#   read the stored bytes into), the record it converts into (LANDING_TO),
#   and the device's word (LANDING_DONE): the step's low word, stored with
#   release by the threadgroup that places the entry's last item, after a
#   system-scope fence following every item's stores, so the host knows the
#   entry's source record read and frees it. The host never resets DONE: it
#   compares it with the step's low word. A failed read is never appended:
#   the host answers its choices -1 and appends nothing.
# The launch's threadgroups claim (entry, item) work through a device
# counter in landing order, each parking only on the host's count for the
# entry it claimed (`streaming/land.hpp`), and stop once the launch's slot
# is answered with the count below their claim: past the last read byte,
# only the last record's conversion remains.
const LANDING_HEAD = 0
const LANDING_ENTRIES = 2
const LANDING_ENTRY_WORDS = 3
const LANDING_FROM = 0
const LANDING_TO = 1
const LANDING_DONE = 2

# A landing launch's counters (device words, `scratch counters`): the claims
# taken, the groups arrived, the polling lead, the count heard, then a word
# per entry counting its items placed, so a launch of L entries holds
# 4 * (LAND_ITEMS + L) bytes.
const LAND_CLAIMS = 0
const LAND_ARRIVALS = 1
const LAND_LEAD = 2
const LAND_HEARD = 3
const LAND_ITEMS = 4

# The landing launch's threadgroups where the device converts (a decode
# layer whose bank places, every scratch round), every backend's: a ceiling
# on the groups claiming (entry, item) work, which a record offers in the
# hundreds of items, so the work never limits it. It is not fitted to a
# device: how many groups are co-resident changes only how fast a convert
# runs, never what it lands (a claim is taken only by a running threadgroup
# and a parked one waits only on the host), and the assessment prices a
# convert by running this launch as built (`routed_land`'s convert point),
# so no other number is owed to the model. It is not derived from the
# device's compute units because the kernels' launches declare
# `threadgroups (LAND_GROUPS, ...)` from this module constant, before any
# device's count is known; a device with more units than this (an 80-core Apple
# Ultra, a large discrete GPU) leaves the rest idle during a convert.
# Where the host tier places, a decode landing converts nothing and is one
# threadgroup.
const LAND_GROUPS = 64

# The scratch kind: a scratch-served step's request at a layer and its
# rounds, the layer's table `scratch[FLIGHT, SCRATCH_SLOT LANES]` (LANES =
# SCRATCH_ROUND + R), step q's slot q mod FLIGHT of lanes of SCRATCH_SLOT words
# each:
# - lane SCRATCH_ASK, the request (`routed_scratch_request`, its experts in
#   its chain place's flags). The answer's payload, the count of records its
#   rounds convert (SCRATCH_STAGED), is the host's before it answers round
#   0; its answer is the step once that count is final. SCRATCH_FOLLOWED,
#   which the host stores before it queues the step's fill, is nonzero
#   where the next launch on the fill's queue is a sibling fill, whose
#   arrival proves this fill's rounds done as an arrival after its last
#   round would (`routed_scratch_grids`).
# - lane SCRATCH_ROUND + r, round r's (`routed_round`): its request is the
#   device's arrival at the round, which its launch stores as it begins;
#   its answer, every record of the round appended to the round's landing
#   list. The host answers every round of a request, the rounds without
#   records too, and a failure fails every round not yet answered, the
#   last always among them. The device arrives at every round the host awaits its arrival
#   at: each with records, and every round where round 0's wait ended on
#   its still pulse before the request's answer (`routed_scratch_grids`),
#   so a host serving a step's rounds in order never waits on an arrival
#   that an expired wait skipped.
const SCRATCH_ASK = 0
const SCRATCH_ROUND = 1
const SCRATCH_STAGED = 8
const SCRATCH_FOLLOWED = 9
const SCRATCH_SLOT = 10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rest of expert streaming's words.

# A ring `ring[RING]`, a hint ring's or a routing record's: the sequence
# (the step's low word) and the count, then its entries from RING_EXPERTS. A
# routing record's are its choices' experts, RING >= RING_EXPERTS + M K (each
# choice's source after them, `routing` below); a hint ring's are pairs,
# RING >= RING_EXPERTS + HINT_ENTRY_WORDS M K: each hinted expert
# (HINT_EXPERT) and the rank of the choice it was hinted as (HINT_RANK, 0 a
# row's best), by which the host keeps each hint's precision, whatever the
# rows.
const RING_SEQUENCE = 0
const RING_COUNT = 1
const RING_EXPERTS = 2
const HINT_ENTRY_WORDS = 2
const HINT_EXPERT = 0
const HINT_RANK = 1

# A layer's routing records `routing[FLIGHT, ROUTING]`, ROUTING >=
# RING_EXPERTS + 2 P, one for each step in flight (the host's bound on steps
# begun and not ended), the one of a step's sequence mod FLIGHT: a ring's
# words, then from RING_EXPERTS + P each choice's source as the route
# resolved it (SOURCE_RECORD's word, a CPU choice's -1). Sequences are
# consecutive, so no step's record is rewritten before the host has read it.

# A list `list[LIST_ENTRIES + LIST_ENTRY_WORDS L]` (`routed_copy`,
# `routed_move`, `routed_seed_commit`): the sequence it was written for and
# the count, then each entry's from and to words.
const LIST_SEQUENCE = 0
const LIST_COUNT = 1
const LIST_ENTRIES = 2
const LIST_ENTRY_WORDS = 2
const LIST_FROM = 0
const LIST_TO = 1

# A group's words of a list it reads (streaming/list.hpp): the pair of the
# entry it visits (LIST_GROUP_PAIR, its from and to words), the first entry
# of the chunk it staged (LIST_GROUP_FIRST) and the chunk's count
# (LIST_GROUP_STAGED), then from LIST_GROUP_CHUNK the chunk's entries, at
# most LIST_CHUNK.
const LIST_GROUP_PAIR = 0
const LIST_GROUP_FIRST = 2
const LIST_GROUP_STAGED = 3
const LIST_GROUP_CHUNK = 4
const LIST_CHUNK = 32

# A list's launch grid `grid[3]` (`routed_list_grid`), on which `routed_copy`
# copies its entries: a row of `grid[0]` threadgroups, the copy's blocks,
# which its host writes once (a copy within device memory LIST_GROUPS by
# LIST_ROWS; a copy over the link the fewest that reach the link's rate,
# `assessment::TierCopyCosts`), while the step's list has entries; none for
# a list another step wrote or one of no entries, so a copy of nothing
# launches nothing. Each threadgroup copies
# its own LIST_PART-byte parts of the listed records in turn, apart from
# every other, so a copy over the link keeps every group's reads in flight
# (an RTX 3090, PCIe 3.0 x16, 2026-11-02, the list staged in device memory:
# 576 KiB records from host memory at 9.6 GB/s for 4 records to 12.6 GB/s
# for 192, against the copy engine's 9.9 and 12.2 and the link's ~13).
const LIST_GROUPS = 64
const LIST_ROWS = 8
const LIST_PART = 16384

# The threads of the protocol entries' one-group launches, every backend's
# (unmeasured: each launch moves a few words, so no backend's choice is a
# measured one): a launch whose work is one thread's (a step word,
# a round's grids; SERIAL_THREADS), a group reading or staging a list or a
# map, or copying listed records (LIST_THREADS, also `routed_copy`'s and
# `routed_move`'s groups), and the scratch request's group, a thread a
# choice of the widest decode (REQUEST_THREADS).
const SERIAL_THREADS = 32
const LIST_THREADS = 256
const REQUEST_THREADS = 1024
